@@ -0,0 +1,17 @@
+# Proven versus assumed ledger
+
+| Boundary | Role and dependents | Classification and evidence |
+|---|---|---|
+| K 7.1.293 reachability engine and built-in `INT`/`BOOL`/K-sequence/configuration semantics | Supplies integer arithmetic, comparison, nonzero remainder, Boolean logic, function evaluation, symbolic rewriting, and circularity handling for every claim. | Accepted low-level trusted primitive. Version is recorded in `03-toolchain.log`; fresh definitions and claims were rebuilt. |
+| Trusted `/reference/py2mpy.py` | Bridges CPython parsing of `solution.py` to the submitted constructor term. | Launcher-trusted input. Candidate copy is byte-identical and trusted regeneration is byte-identical to `solution.mpy` (`01-integrity.log`, `02-translation.log`). |
+| Exact S3 whole-program lowering in `semantic.k` | Connects the actual constructor tree and `<n>` argument to the `scan`/`trial` transition system; the entry theorem depends on it. | Faithful by exhaustive operation/control mapping, three-way parsed constructor identity, body sensitivity, and finite K/Python comparisons. It is not an opaque result oracle and contains no specification symbol. It remains an informal, monolithic generated-semantics bridge without a separate compositional Python semantics or bridge-free connection theorem, so it is a non-fatal concern (`04-program-pinning.log`, `04-body-mutation-*`, `05-rule-inventory.md`). |
+| `scan`/`trial` transition rules | Execute candidate enumeration, divisor testing, flag update, increments, ordered accumulation, and return. | Formally related to `primesFrom`/`noFactor` by the reconstructed C1/C2 reachability claims. Static guards are disjoint/exhaustive on the reachable domain; 16 fresh K executions agree with both Python implementations (`03-concrete-compare.log`). |
+| `noFactor`/`isPrime` mathematics | Gives the meaning of “prime” used in the postcondition. | Visible terminating equations on the theorem domain plus the ordinary divisor-bound lemma: every composite `C >= 2` has a factor at most `sqrt(C)`. No opaque value or task-answer axiom. |
+| `primesFrom`/`primesBelow` and `PList` | Gives the ascending result sequence and represents a Python list as `nil`/`cons`. | Visible recursive equations. Structural sequence/order bridge is direct and supported by canonical differential tests. Python allocation identity is unobservable in this program. |
+| `[total]` on `noFactor` and `primesFrom` | Backend declaration for proof-side functions. | Equations cover every proof use (`C,D >= 2` for `noFactor`; `C >= 2` for `primesFrom`) but not their entire declared sorts. `noFactor(0,0)` and `primesFrom(0,2)` expose off-domain equation holes. No intended-domain state reaches a hole; recorded as an over-broad declaration concern, not a false theorem-domain conclusion. |
+| Python/canonical differential evidence | Checks implementation fidelity and the generated-semantics bridge on finite inputs. | 104 candidate/canonical inputs and 16 K/candidate/canonical inputs have zero mismatches. This is finite empirical support only, never a substitute for C1-C3 (`02-differential.log`, `03-concrete-compare.log`). |
+| Termination | The source loops terminate for each finite non-negative integer by increasing bounded `candidate` and `divisor`. | The requested theorem is partial correctness, so termination is outside the formal result. No conclusion relies on treating nontermination as a returned value. |
+
+There are no fresh result variables, uninterpreted/opaque result-bearing
+symbols, trusted task answers, external I/O, heap aliases, exceptions, or
+proof-local priority rules.
